<!DOCTYPE html>
<html metal:use-macro="context/@@pfas-macros/macros/page"
      xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
      xmlns:tal="http://xml.zope.org/namespaces/tal"
      xmlns:metal="http://xml.zope.org/namespaces/metal">
<head>
  <title metal:fill-slot="title">Lab Settings</title>
  <tal:styles metal:fill-slot="head-extra">
  <style>
    /* Layout, plus the two alert boxes. Every STRUCTURAL colour is a theme
       token, and the status pills reuse the global .badge-* classes rather than
       redefining them (§6C: define UI once).
       The two hex pairs below are NOT tokens, and that is worth stating plainly
       rather than claiming otherwise: var(--s-danger-lt)/var(--s-danger-dk) and var(--s-success-lt)/var(--s-success-text) are the
       same danger/success pairs the global .badge-expired and .badge-custom use
       and that every Facility QC page repeats inline. Consolidating that pair
       into theme tokens would touch a dozen templates, so it is its own cleanup,
       not something to smuggle in here. */
    .ls-why { background:var(--s-light); border-left:3px solid var(--s-border);
              padding:10px 14px; font-size:var(--fs-sm); color:var(--s-secondary);
              margin-bottom:16px; border-radius:0 4px 4px 0; }
    .ls-bar { display:flex; gap:10px; flex-wrap:wrap; align-items:flex-end;
              background:var(--s-bg); border:1px solid var(--s-border);
              border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin-bottom:16px; }
    .ls-fld { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:3px; }
    .ls-fld label { font-size:var(--fs-xs); font-weight:600; color:var(--s-secondary); }
    .ls-fld input, .ls-fld select { padding:6px 8px; font-size:var(--fs-base);
              border:1px solid var(--s-input-border); border-radius:4px;
              color:var(--s-input-text); }
    .ls-alert { display:block; padding:10px 14px; border-radius:6px;
                margin-bottom:16px; font-size:var(--fs-base); font-weight:600;
                background:var(--s-danger-lt); color:var(--s-danger-dk); text-decoration:none; }
    .ls-ok { background:var(--s-success-lt); color:var(--s-success-text); font-weight:400; }
    .ls-group { border:1px solid var(--s-border); border-radius:8px;
                margin-bottom:14px; background:var(--s-bg); }
    .ls-count { font-weight:400; color:var(--s-secondary); font-size:var(--fs-sm); }
    .ls-key { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size:var(--fs-xs); color:var(--s-secondary); }
    .ls-val { font-weight:600; color:var(--s-body); }
    .ls-seed { color:var(--s-secondary); font-size:var(--fs-xs); }
    .ls-judge { font-size:var(--fs-xs); font-weight:700; color:var(--s-danger-dk); }
    .ls-empty { padding:12px 14px; font-size:var(--fs-sm); color:var(--s-secondary); }
    /* Wide table scrolls inside its own box, as every other PFAS table does. */
    .ls-scroll { overflow-x:auto; }
  </style>
  </tal:styles>
</head>
<body>
<tal:t metal:fill-slot="header-title">Lab Settings</tal:t>
<metal:content metal:fill-slot="content">
  <tal:def tal:define="purl view/portal_url;
                       groups view/groups;
                       unset_n view/unconfigured_count;
                       total view/total_count">

  <div class="ls-why">
    Every setting the laboratory owns, in one place, enumerated from the settings
    registry rather than a maintained list &mdash; so a newly declared setting
    appears here without anyone remembering to add it. Each row says whether it is
    still the <strong>default in code</strong>, has been <strong>customised</strong>
    by the lab, or is <strong>not configured</strong>. Settings are edited in the
    page that owns them, linked on the right: this console lists and never writes,
    so there is exactly one owner for each fact.
  </div>

  <!-- The count that matters. A judging criterion with no value does not fall
       back to a plausible number — it refuses, and the batch cannot be released.
       Until now nothing told a lab WHICH criterion. -->
  <div tal:condition="unset_n" class="ls-alert">
    &#9888; <tal:n tal:replace="unset_n">2</tal:n> setting(s) are NOT CONFIGURED.
    A judging criterion with no value blocks release rather than assuming one.
    <a class="btn-sm btn-secondary"
       tal:attributes="href string:${purl}/@@pfas-lab-settings?status=unset">Show me which</a>
  </div>
  <div tal:condition="not:unset_n" class="ls-alert ls-ok">
    Every judging criterion has a value.
  </div>

  <form method="GET" class="ls-bar"
        tal:attributes="action string:${purl}/@@pfas-lab-settings">
    <div class="ls-fld" style="flex:1;min-width:200px">
      <label for="q">Search</label>
      <input type="text" name="q" id="q" placeholder="holding time, tolerance, wording&hellip;"
             tal:attributes="value view/query" />
    </div>
    <div class="ls-fld">
      <label for="tier">Who may edit</label>
      <select name="tier" id="tier">
        <option value="">Anyone who may configure</option>
        <option tal:repeat="opt view/tier_options"
                tal:attributes="value python:opt[0];
                                selected python:view.tier_filter() == opt[0]"
                tal:content="python:opt[1]">Lab</option>
      </select>
    </div>
    <div class="ls-fld">
      <label for="status">Status</label>
      <select name="status" id="status">
        <option value="">Any status</option>
        <option tal:repeat="opt view/status_options"
                tal:attributes="value python:opt[0];
                                selected python:view.status_filter() == opt[0]"
                tal:content="python:opt[1]">Default</option>
      </select>
    </div>
    <div class="ls-fld">
      <label>&nbsp;</label>
      <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Filter</button>
    </div>
    <div class="ls-fld" tal:condition="view/filtering">
      <label>&nbsp;</label>
      <a class="btn" tal:attributes="href string:${purl}/@@pfas-lab-settings">Clear</a>
    </div>
  </form>

  <!-- One tab per settings group (: tabs, not a long accordion).
       The first group with rows under the current filter opens first; counts
       stay shown-of-total so a filtered tab never reads as the whole group. -->
  <tal:first tal:define="firstid python:([g['id'] for g in groups if g['shown']] or [groups[0]['id'] if groups else ''])[0]">
  <div class="pfas-tabs" data-tabs="">
    <a tal:repeat="g groups"
       tal:attributes="class python:'pfas-tab active' if g['id'] == firstid else 'pfas-tab';
                       data-pane string:ls-${g/id};
                       href string:#ls-${g/id}">
      <tal:l tal:replace="g/label">Group</tal:l><span class="pfas-tab-count"><tal:c
        tal:condition="python:g['shown'] == g['total']">(<tal:n tal:replace="g/total">0</tal:n>)</tal:c><tal:c
        tal:condition="python:g['shown'] != g['total']">(<tal:n tal:replace="g/shown">0</tal:n> of <tal:t tal:replace="g/total">0</tal:t>)</tal:c></span><span
        class="pfas-tab-flag" tal:condition="g/unset"
        tal:attributes="title string:${g/unset} not configured">&#9888; <tal:n tal:replace="g/unset">1</tal:n></span>
    </a>
  </div>
  </tal:first>

  <tal:groups tal:repeat="g groups">
    <div class="pfas-pane ls-group" tal:attributes="id string:ls-${g/id}">

      <div class="ls-scroll" tal:condition="g/rows">
        <table class="pfas-table ls-table">
          <thead><tr>
            <th>Setting</th><th>Value</th><th>Default</th>
            <th>Status</th><th>Who may edit</th><th>Edit in</th>
          </tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tal:rows tal:repeat="r g/rows">
            <tr>
              <td>
                <div class="ls-val" tal:content="r/label">Label</div>
                <div class="ls-judge" tal:condition="r/judging">
                  decides pass / fail
                </div>
                <div class="ls-seed" tal:condition="r/help|nothing"
                     tal:content="r/help"></div>
              </td>
              <td>
                <span class="ls-val" tal:content="r/display_value">value</span>
                <span class="ls-seed" tal:condition="r/unit|nothing"
                      tal:content="r/unit"></span>
              </td>
              <td class="ls-seed">
                <tal:s tal:condition="r/has_seed" tal:content="r/display_seed">seed</tal:s>
                <tal:s tal:condition="not:r/has_seed">no default &mdash; must be set</tal:s>
              </td>
              <td>
                <!-- a tag only for something to act on -->
                <span tal:condition="python:r['status_class'] in ('badge-expired', 'badge-quarantine')"
                      tal:attributes="class string:badge ${r/status_class}"
                      tal:content="r/status_label">NOT CONFIGURED</span>
                <span tal:condition="python:r['status_class'] not in ('badge-expired', 'badge-quarantine')"
                      tal:content="r/status_label">Default</span>
              </td>
              <td class="ls-seed"
                  tal:content="python:'Lab manager / QAO' if r['tier'] == 'lab' else 'System admin'">Lab</td>
              <td>
                <a class="btn-sm btn-secondary" tal:condition="r/owner_href"
                   tal:attributes="href r/owner_href; title string:${r/owner_view}">Open editor</a>
                <span tal:condition="not:r/owner_href" class="ls-seed">
                  this page
                </span>
              </td>
            </tr>
            </tal:rows>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <!-- An empty group says WHY it is empty. A blank section reads as a bug. -->
      <div class="ls-empty" tal:condition="not:g/rows">
        <tal:filtered tal:condition="python:g['total'] and view.filtering()">
          No setting in this group matches the current filter.
        </tal:filtered>
        <tal:none tal:condition="python:not g['total']">
          Nothing is declared in this group yet. Its values still live in code, so
          changing one is a code change today &mdash; they are migrated here one at
          a time, each together with the code that reads it, so the console can
          never list a setting that changes nothing.
        </tal:none>
      </div>
    </div>
  </tal:groups>

  <p class="ls-seed" style="margin-top:14px">
    <tal:t tal:replace="total">0</tal:t> settings declared. Values are read from
    the page that owns each one, so what you see here is what the software uses.
  </p>

  </tal:def>
</metal:content>
</body>
</html>
